    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d174">

                <pb facs="0221" n="207" sameAs="frus:frus1944v07/pg207"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">838.154/9–2644</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Haiti</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Wilson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 247</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Port-au-Prince</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-09-26">September 26,
                        1944</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to report that the work
                    of the United States Public Roads Administration has been formally terminated in
                    Haiti. Mr. Frank H. Gardner, Resident Engineer, has provided this Embassy with a
                    copy of his letter of September 15, 1944, to the Secretary of State for Public
                    Works, in which he states that the Public Roads Administration cannot approve
                    any additional work on the Morne-à-Cabrit road.</p>
                <p>The termination of this work occasioned some unwarranted comment in <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Le Nouvelliste</hi> of September 19. This paper said
                    that the few Haitians employed by the Public Roads Administration became
                    unemployed automatically, that Haiti did not have time to benefit to any extent
                    from the project, and that the idea of Pan American roads was to unite the
                    American Republics.</p>
                <p>This comment fails to stress, perhaps deliberately, the essential fact about the
                    closing of the office of the Public Roads Administration in <pb facs="0222"
                        n="208" xml:id="pg208"/> Haiti. The office was opened, not to assist the
                    Haitians, but to construct roads which would permit transport of goods to Puerto
                    Rico, with a maximum of freedom from submarine attack, thus assisting in the
                    prosecution of the war. When the submarine menace virtually disappeared, there
                    was no further need for an alternative to an all sea route to Puerto Rico, and
                    consequently, the Public Roads Administration closed its doors in Haiti.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Orme Wilson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
